> But I don't think this PR can work. The basic understanding of the bug is: we load the static code twice and we access the static variables from both places. With your patch, we have two bus objects, but we still access the same static storage from both variables, incl. shared_hash_key. So one module initializes the memory for the static variables, scribbles into shared_hash_key, and creates a bus object. The other module scribbles into shared_hash_key and creates a second bus object. So your patch "unshares" a part of the state, but not all of it, so it just moves the problem around. IIUC.

We load the static code twice, but their static variables should be duplicated really then, if linked in twice so that should not be a problem.

@bluca's patch only deals with the fact that the PAM context currently is the point where we end up sharing the pointer between both modules after all, and splits that in two.

review_comments:
I'd still prefer if we looked at the actual condition here, i.e. at the pid, not the flag. 

so what about this: we do what we do with sd-bus and attach a PID to pam_handle_t. i.e. with some helper func we'll store the pid in a data field, and then we check it again whenever needed. i.e.:

```c
int pam_validate_pid(pam_handle_t h) {
        const void *p;
        int r;

        /* If this function wasn't called on the handle yet, installs the current PID on it. Otherwise checks if the installed PID matches our own, and if not returns PAM_BAD_ITEM. */

        r = pam_get_data(handle, "systemd-pid", &p);
        if (r == PAM_NO_MODULE_DATA) 
                return pam_set_data(handle, "systemd-pid", PID_TO_PTR(getpid_cached()), NULL);
        if (r != PAM_SUCCESS) 
                return r;

        return PTR_TO_PID(p) == getpid_cached() ? PAM_SUCCESS : PAM_BAD_ITEM;
}
```

And then in all our pam hooks, just add first thing:

```c
r = pam_validate_pid(h);
if (r != PAM_SUCCESS)
        return r;
```

